### How do you check the requirement?

Start by reading the ticket or user story, then the PR description, then the diff. Confirm the change does exactly what was asked, nothing less and nothing more. Scope creep -- sneaking in unrelated edits -- makes reviews and rollbacks harder even if the extra code is good.

```java
// Ticket NBI-456: Add daily transfer limit of 10 lakh for SAV accounts
// The PR should add ONLY the limit check, not refactor the whole service

package com.bank.payments;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {

    private static final BigDecimal DAILY_LIMIT =
        new BigDecimal("1000000.00");

    public void transfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount,
                         BigDecimal amount) {
        if (amount.compareTo(DAILY_LIMIT) > 0) {
            throw new DailyLimitExceededException(
                "Transfer of " + amount + " exceeds daily limit");
        }
        // ... proceed with transfer
    }
}
```

### How do you check business logic?

Trace the logic by hand against realistic cases. In banking, money must always use BigDecimal, never double. Look hard at boundary conditions -- zero amounts, exactly-at-the-limit values, negative inputs.

```java
package com.bank.payments;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferValidator {

    // WRONG -- using double for money
    public boolean isValidAmount_WRONG(double amount) {
        return amount > 0 && amount <= 1000000.00;
        // 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.30000000000000004 in double!
    }

    // CORRECT -- using BigDecimal
    public boolean isValidAmount(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) > 0
            && amount.compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000000.00")) <= 0;
    }
}
```

```text
Review comment: "amount > 0" -- what about exactly zero?
Review comment: "<= 1000000" -- the spec says 'strictly less than 10 lakh'
  or 'up to and including 10 lakh'? Please clarify with the ticket.
```

### How do you check exception handling?

Exceptions should be specific, transactions should roll back cleanly on error, and error messages must not leak sensitive data. For a bank, failing closed (rejecting) is safer than failing open (allowing).

```java
package com.bank.payments;

import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferService {

    // WRONG -- catches everything, swallows the error, leaks account details
    @Transactional
    public void transfer_WRONG(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            // ... transfer logic
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println("Failed for account " + from
                + " balance " + getBalance(from)); // leaks balance!
        }
    }

    // CORRECT -- specific exception, clean rollback, no sensitive data
    @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            // ... transfer logic
        } catch (InsufficientFundsException e) {
            throw e; // let it propagate, transaction rolls back
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new TransferFailedException(
                "Transfer failed for request " + from + " -> " + to, e);
            // No balance or sensitive data in the message
        }
    }

    private BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        return BigDecimal.ZERO; // placeholder
    }
}
```

### How do you check security?

Security is critical in banking. Check for parameterized queries, input validation, authorization so a user cannot act on another account, no hardcoded secrets, and sensitive data not logged.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import org.springframework.data.repository.query.Param;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, Long> {

    // WRONG -- string concatenation, SQL injection vulnerability
    // "SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = '" + accountId + "'"

    // CORRECT -- parameterized query
    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.accountId = :accountId")
    Account findByAccountId(@Param("accountId") String accountId);
}
```

```text
Review comment (BLOCKER): Line 15 concatenates user input into SQL.
  This is a SQL injection vulnerability. Use @Query with :param binding.
```

### How do you check performance?

Watch for N+1 queries, loading unbounded data into memory, and holding connections or locks too long. Do not demand premature optimization, but flag anything that will not scale to production volumes.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Transaction;
import java.util.List;

public class ReportService {

    // WRONG -- N+1: one query per account
    public void generateReport_WRONG(List<Account> accounts) {
        for (Account acct : accounts) {
            List<Transaction> txns = txnRepo.findByAccountId(acct.getId());
            // 1000 accounts = 1001 queries!
        }
    }

    // CORRECT -- batch query
    public void generateReport(List<String> accountIds) {
        List<Transaction> txns = txnRepo.findByAccountIdIn(accountIds);
        // 1 query regardless of account count
    }
}
```

### How do you check tests?

Confirm there are tests for the new logic covering the happy path, important failure cases, and boundaries. Assertions should be meaningful. If a bug is being fixed, there should be a test that would have caught it.

```java
package com.bank.payments;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

class TransferValidatorTest {

    private final TransferValidator validator = new TransferValidator();

    @Test
    void validAmount_withinLimit() {
        assertTrue(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("50000.00")));
    }

    @Test
    void validAmount_exactlyAtLimit() {
        assertTrue(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("1000000.00")));
    }

    @Test
    void invalidAmount_zero() {
        assertFalse(validator.isValidAmount(BigDecimal.ZERO));
    }

    @Test
    void invalidAmount_negative() {
        assertFalse(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("-100.00")));
    }

    @Test
    void invalidAmount_exceedsLimit() {
        assertFalse(validator.isValidAmount(new BigDecimal("1000001.00")));
    }
}
```

